1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned system that records medical claims, their evidence, their replication status, and their revisions. A medical claim is a statement about health, disease, treatment, prevention, diagnosis, or prognosis. The Medical Claim Verifier records each claim with its description, its condition, its intervention, its outcome, the evidence for it, the evidence against it, its replication status, its revisions, and its provenance.

It reuses:

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Full-Pipeline Simulator (App 9)

the Null-Model Protocol Library (App 18)

the STAG Audit Tool (App 21)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22)

the Replication Tracker (App 26)

the Evidence Aggregator (App 46)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for clinicians, medical researchers, journal editors, patients, and anyone who wants to know what the evidence for a medical claim actually is.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of medical claims from users.

Store each claim with its description, its condition, its intervention, its outcome, and its provenance.

Record the evidence for the claim: the studies, the trials, the meta-analyses, the sample sizes, the effect sizes, the confidence intervals.

Record the evidence against the claim: contrary studies, null results, safety concerns, methodological criticisms.

Record the replication status: not_replicated, replicated, partially_replicated, failed_replication, or uncertain.

Record the revisions: how the claim has changed over time, and why.

Record the level of evidence: systematic_review, randomized_controlled_trial, cohort_study, case_control_study, case_series, case_report, expert_opinion, or other.

Record the grade of recommendation: strong_for, weak_for, weak_against, strong_against, or uncertain.

Record the population and the clinical setting.

Record the safety profile and the adverse events.

Record the conflicts of interest and the funding sources.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by claim ID, description, condition, intervention, outcome, replication status, level of evidence, grade, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the claim description, the evidence, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by condition, by intervention, by level of evidence, by grade, by replication status, by year, by funder.

Support exporting a claim as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a claim from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking a claim to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the claim is described in a deposited work.

Support linking a claim to a Full-Pipeline Simulator result if the claim has been tested statistically.

Support linking a claim to a Null-Model Protocol result.

Support linking a claim to a Numerical Verification Tool result.

Support linking a claim to a Replication Tracker record.

Support the claim map: a visualisation of claims by condition and by intervention.

Support the evidence hierarchy view: a visualisation of the evidence for and against a claim, ordered by level of evidence.

Support the replication funnel: a visualisation of the path from the original study to the replication.

Support the safety profile view: a visualisation of the adverse events and the safety signals.

Support the revision timeline: a visualisation of how a claim has changed over time.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical claim in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 claims.

Versioned: the system records the STAG schema version, the Null-Model Protocol schema version, the medical claim schema version, and the system version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

